1
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:46,000
From a place you never heard of...a story you'll never forget.

2
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:26,000
Gallipoli is a war, history, drama film directed by Peter Weir.

3
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:06,000
Two Australian sprinters face the brutal realities of war when they are sent to fight
in the Gallipoli campaign in the Ottoman Empire during World War I.

4
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:46,000
Frank Dunne, played by Mel Gibson.

5
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:26,000
Archy Hamilton, played by Mark Lee.

6
00:28:00,000 --> 00:28:06,000
Jack, played by Bill Kerr.

7
00:32:40,000 --> 00:32:46,000
Les McCann, played by Harold Hopkins.

8
00:37:20,000 --> 00:37:26,000
Zac, played by Charles Lathalu Yunipingu.

9
00:42:00,000 --> 00:42:06,000
Stockman, played by Heath Harris.

10
00:46:40,000 --> 00:46:46,000
Gallipoli is a 1981 Australian war drama film directed by Peter Weir and produced by
Patricia Lovell and Robert Stigwood, starring Mel Gibson and Mark Lee.

11
00:51:20,000 --> 00:51:26,000
The film revolves around several young men from Western Australia
who enlist in the Australian Army during World War I.

12
00:56:00,000 --> 00:56:06,000
They are sent to the Gallipoli peninsula in the Ottoman Empire (modern-day
Turkey), where they take part in the Gallipoli campaign.

13
01:00:40,000 --> 01:00:46,000
During the course of the film, the young men slowly lose
their innocence about the purpose of war.

14
01:05:20,000 --> 01:05:26,000
The climax of the film occurs on the Anzac battlefield at Gallipoli, depicting
the futile attack at the Battle of the Nek on 7 August 1915.

15
01:10:00,000 --> 01:10:06,000
Gallipoli, which had a budget of $2.6 million, provides a faithful portrayal of life in Australia in the 1910s—reminiscent of Weir's 1975 film Picnic at Hanging Rock set in 1900—and captures the ideals and
character of the Australians who joined up to fight, as well as the conditions they endured on the battlefield, although its portrayal of British forces has been criticised as inaccurate.

16
01:14:40,000 --> 01:14:46,000
It followed the Australian New Wave war film Breaker Morant (1980) and preceded
the 5-part TV series Anzacs (1985), and The Lighthorsemen (1987).

17
01:19:20,000 --> 01:19:26,000
Themes of these films include the Australian identity, such as mateship and larrikinism, the loss of innocence in war,
and the continued coming of age of the Australian nation and its soldiers (later called the Anzac spirit).

18
01:24:00,000 --> 01:24:06,000
Gallipoli received heavy international promotion and distribution and helped to elevate the worldwide
reputation of the Australian film industry and of later Australian New Wave films.

19
01:28:40,000 --> 01:28:46,000
The film also helped to launch the international career of actor Mel Gibson.

20
01:33:20,000 --> 01:33:26,000
Due to the Gallipoli battlefields becoming tourist destinations in the 21st century, the film is
often shown at the hostels and hotels in Eceabat and Çanakkale on the Dardanelles.

21
01:38:00,000 --> 01:38:06,000
In the 20 to 1 episode "Great Aussie Films", Gallipoli was listed as Number 1.

22
01:42:40,000 --> 01:42:46,000
The film explores themes of australia, army, world war
i, battle assignment, british empire.

23
01:47:20,000 --> 01:47:26,000
The tone is dark and emotional.
